findings.body:
Before this PR, a failure in `ConversationVariableUpdaterImpl.update` raised inside `_run()`, so the node failed and the workflow saw the error. Now the write happens in a layer. `EventManager._notify_layers` wraps every `layer.on_event` call in `try/except Exception` and only logs it (`event_manager.py:182-186`). So if the row is missing, the connection fails, or the commit fails, the node still reports `SUCCEEDED`, the in-memory pool already holds the new value, and the DB keeps the old one. Later turns in the same conversation read the stale value.

Multi-variable assignments make this worse. Each `update()` commits on its own, so a failure partway through leaves some variables written and others not, and the node still reports success.

::diff{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="30-53"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The loop calls `update()` per variable with no transaction and no failure propagation. Any exception here is invisible to the workflow.

The original `ConversationVariableUpdaterImpl.update` used `with Session(db.engine) as session:`. The moved version creates `session = Session(db.engine)` and never closes it. On the success path, `commit()` does not release the session's connection. On the `not row` path, the function raises before any cleanup. In both cases the connection is only returned to the pool when the session is garbage-collected. Every conversation variable update opens one session, so under load this exhausts the connection pool. It also leaks on the error path, which is now the path that matters most (see the previous finding).

::diff{file="api/services/conversation_variable_updater.py" lines="14-23"}

::suggestion{file="api/services/conversation_variable_updater.py" lines="18-23"}
with Session(db.engine) as session:
    row = session.scalar(stmt)
    if not row:
        raise ConversationVariableNotFoundError("conversation variable not found in the database")
    row.data = variable.model_dump_json()
    session.commit()

In v2, a missing `conversation_id` used to raise `ConversationIDNotFoundError` for every run that was not in the debugger, so the workflow failed loudly. The layer now returns early when `conversation_id is None`, and the node still reports success with no persistence. The `ConversationIDNotFoundError` class in `v2/exc.py` is now unused. The debugger exemption also disappears, so a debugger run that previously tolerated the missing id now silently drops the write.

This is probably rare in practice, since advanced-chat runs normally carry a conversation id, but the behaviour change is silent and is not called out in the PR description or tests.

::diff{file="api/core/app/layers/conversation_variable_persist_layer.py" lines="30-36"}
